  {
    "claim": "agent.llm.claude_code is a delegating sub-agent, not a drop-in model provider: it always answers kind:text and never kind:tool_calls.",
    "detail": "chat_llm's other arms answer ONE model turn and hand tool calls back for tool_loop to execute. Claude Code is an agent harness that runs its own loop with its own tools and returns only when the work is done, so tool_loop terminates on its answer - the turn was delegated wholesale and the text IS the finished result. The `tools` newbound passes are therefore ignored (they name commands only the newbound process can run). To give the delegate real capability, point CLAUDE_CODE_MCP at newbound's own MCP server, which exposes every store command rather than the subset tool_loop forwarded.",
    "tags": "llm,claude-code,llm-ctl,bridge",
    "source": {
      "doc": "agent.llm.claude_code"
    },
    "confidence": "high",
    "time": 1786882401239
  },
  {
    "claim": "Replacing Claude Code's default system prompt and disabling its built-in tools makes a bridged turn roughly 170x cheaper.",
    "detail": "Measured on a trivial prompt: the default configuration loaded ~38,000 cache-creation tokens of Claude Code system prompt plus CLAUDE.md discovery ($0.229 notional), against ~200 input tokens ($0.0013) with --system-prompt replacing it and --tools \"\" disabling built-ins. claude_code therefore defaults to replace-mode, mapping the conversation's own system messages onto --system-prompt. CLAUDE_CODE_SYSTEM_MODE=append keeps Claude Code's prompt and pays for it, which is what agentic delegation actually wants.",
    "tags": "llm,claude-code,cost",
    "source": {
      "doc": "agent.llm.claude_code"
    },
    "confidence": "high",
    "time": 1786882401312
  },
  {
    "claim": "The prompt must ride stdin, never argv, because Claude Code's variadic flags silently swallow a positional prompt.",
    "detail": "--tools, --allowedTools, --disallowedTools and --mcp-config are declared <tools...> style, so any following non-option argument is consumed as another value: `claude -p --tools \"\" \"my prompt\"` fails with \"Input must be provided either through stdin or as a prompt argument\". stdin also sidesteps ARG_MAX on long conversations. claude_code additionally emits --tools LAST so nothing it appends can be eaten.",
    "tags": "llm,claude-code,cli",
    "source": {
      "doc": "agent.llm.claude_code"
    },
    "confidence": "high",
    "time": 1786882401387
  },
  {
    "claim": "Reaping a timed-out `claude` needs BOTH an explicit process group and `kill -9 -- -PID`; either alone leaves the tree running.",
    "detail": "`claude` is a node launcher, so killing the direct child can leave the interpreter alive. The child is spawned with process_group(0) (pgid == pid) and the timeout path signals the negative pid. The `--` is load-bearing and was a real bug: /bin/kill is not the shell builtin and parses a bare `-1234` as options, so the first implementation reported a successful kill while the process kept running - verified by ps five seconds later. Separately, CLAUDE_CODE_PERMISSION_MODE=bypassPermissions is refused outright when the server runs as root (\"cannot be used with root/sudo privileges\").",
    "tags": "llm,claude-code,process,timeout",
    "source": {
      "doc": "agent.llm.claude_code"
    },
    "confidence": "high",
    "time": 1786882401513
  },
